Our delta on top of the `iced_term` 0.8.0 hardcopy.
=======================================================================

`crates/baton-term` started as a verbatim copy of `iced_term` 0.8.0
(MIT, crates.io, git e0e90be2c160bd68f1bf544a51bf772d75b98ffc).
Every line we changed is marked with a `// BATON:` comment in the source;
this file is the machine-readable form of the same thing.

To regenerate:

    curl -sL -o iced_term.crate \
      https://static.crates.io/crates/iced_term/iced_term-0.8.0.crate
    tar xzf iced_term.crate
    for f in crates/baton-term/src/*.rs; do
      diff -u "iced_term-0.8.0/src/$(basename $f)" "$f"
    done

What we changed and why:

  * backend.rs  Split the alacritty event channel in two. `Wakeup` is
                coalesced at the source through a capacity-1 channel;
                everything else goes through an unbounded channel so it
                can never be dropped. The original called `blocking_send`
                while holding `FairMutex<Term>`, which deadlocks: the only
                task that can drain the channel is the UI thread, and the
                UI thread is blocked waiting for that same lock.
                Reproduced 100% of the time with `yes | head -c 100000`.
                Regression test: crates/baton-term/tests/deadlock.rs

  * backend.rs  Damage tracking. `Term::damage()` decides whether the grid
                actually changed; the original cloned the whole
                `Grid<Cell>` on every sync and never looked at damage.

  * view.rs     Glyph run batching. Contiguous same-style ASCII cells are
                shaped once instead of once per cell: 43,108 -> 375
                `fill_text` calls on a dense page, geometry build 672 ->
                313 us.

  * view.rs     Instrumentation (`metrics.rs`, new file) used to measure
                the above.

  * terminal.rs Removed two `panic!`s. A closed channel is the normal way
                a pane shuts down, not a bug. Added `dump_grid()`, which
                is the canonical correctness oracle for rendering.

  * metrics.rs  The queue-depth gauge saturates at both ends instead of
                wrapping. It is read by the PTY reader thread, and an
                unmatched decrement used to wrap to u64::MAX and make the
                next increment overflow -- which panics in a debug build
                and kills that thread. Instrumentation must not be able
                to take down the thread it measures.
                Regression test: crates/baton-term/tests/deadlock.rs

